blocks:
  - type: metin
    baslik: What is PTSD?
    metin: |-
      An accident, a disaster, being the victim or witness of violence, a sudden loss: post-traumatic stress disorder can follow experiences like these. The World Health Organization (WHO) describes PTSD through three groups of symptoms: re-experiencing the event, avoiding reminders of it, and feeling a persistent sense of threat. To speak of PTSD, these symptoms need to cause significant distress and interfere with family, social, school or working life ([WHO, 2026](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/post-traumatic-stress-disorder)).

      What makes an experience traumatic is not how big the event was but the mark it leaves on the person. The same event can leave very different marks on two people. That difference is not a measure of strength or weakness.
  - type: metin
    baslik: Not every hard experience turns into PTSD
    metin: |-
      After a shaking event it is ordinary for sleep to be disturbed, to startle easily, to have the event come back before your eyes, or to feel emotionally numb. The International Society for Traumatic Stress Studies (ISTSS) notes that many people have symptoms similar to PTSD in the weeks after a trauma, but that most people do not have long-term problems. For some people the symptoms do not go away on their own, and PTSD is one possible outcome when that happens ([ISTSS](https://istss.org/public-resources/trauma-basics/natural-recovery-vs-ptsd/)).

      So the reactions of the first weeks do not, on their own, mean a disorder. What matters is what happens as time goes on.
  - type: kart-yigini
    baslik: Symptoms of PTSD
    giris: 'The three groups of symptoms WHO describes can look like this in daily life:'
    kartlar:
      - baslik: Re-experiencing
        metin: The event comes back unbidden and again and again, and feels as if it is happening all over. Sometimes this is a sudden image or sound, sometimes a nightmare. A smell, a place or a sentence can set these memories off.
      - baslik: Avoidance
        metin: Places, people, activities and even thoughts that bring the event to mind are kept at a distance. Avoidance may bring relief in the short term, but over time it can narrow life; a familiar route stops being used, some subjects are never raised.
      - baslik: A sense of threat
        metin: WHO describes this as a heightened sense of danger even when there is no actual risk. It can show as being constantly on guard, startling easily, finding it hard to fall or stay asleep, and struggling to concentrate. For more on the body's stress response, see our article on the [brain–body connection](/en/blog/brain-body-connection.html).
  - type: metin
    baslik: PTSD symptoms in children
    metin: |-
      Young children cannot always put what they have been through into words. WHO notes that in younger children symptoms are often behavioural, and that the event may be re-enacted in play or in drawings ([WHO, 2026](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/post-traumatic-stress-disorder)). Disturbed sleep, difficulty separating from a parent, or slipping back in skills already learned are also changes parents often notice.

      For more detail, see the [child psychology](/en/services/child-psychology.html) and [adolescent psychology](/en/services/adolescent-psychology.html) pages.
  - type: onayli-liste
    baslik: When is it time to get support?
    giris: 'According to WHO, PTSD symptoms usually begin immediately after a traumatic event or within the first month. It may be worth talking to a psychologist if:'
    maddeler:
      - baslik: The symptoms are not easing even after weeks have passed
      - baslik: Sleep, work, school or relationships are clearly affected
      - baslik: Avoidance is steadily narrowing your life
      - baslik: You are turning to alcohol or drugs to cope with what you went through
    kapanis: If you hesitate to call what you went through "trauma", that is not a reason not to get support. For more general signs, see our article on [when to go to therapy](/en/blog/when-to-go-to-therapy.html).
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: turuncu
    baslik: If you are not safe
    metin: If you are having thoughts of harming yourself or you are not safe, go to your nearest emergency department without waiting.
  - type: onayli-liste
    baslik: 'Paths to recovery from PTSD: steps that help in daily life'
    giris: 'Recovering does not mean forgetting what happened. The aim is to reduce the power it has to shape your life today. ISTSS stresses that ensuring physical, emotional and psychological safety is the first and most important step towards recovery ([ISTSS](https://istss.org/public-resources/trauma-basics/getting-help/)). WHO''s advice sits alongside this:'
    maddeler:
      - baslik: Keep up your daily routine as far as you can
      - baslik: Move regularly and do not skip meals
      - baslik: Try to protect your sleep pattern
      - baslik: Avoid or cut down on alcohol and drugs
      - baslik: Make a list of things that do you good, and do at least one each day
    kapanis: Talking to someone you trust can also ease the sense of being alone. You decide how much to tell.
  - type: kart-yigini
    baslik: Psychological support
    giris: 'Trauma-focused work does not hold to a single method. The approaches used at our centre are:'
    kartlar:
      - baslik: Trauma-focused cognitive behavioural therapy
        metin: Structured work on the patterns of thought and avoidance connected with the trauma. [CBT page](/en/services/cbt-therapy.html)
      - baslik: Psychodynamic psychotherapy
        metin: Work that looks at how early experiences are reflected in present-day relationships. [Psychodynamic therapy page](/en/services/psychodynamic-therapy.html)
      - baslik: Acceptance and commitment therapy (ACT)
        metin: An approach that works on living alongside a hard experience and turning towards one's values, rather than fighting it. [ACT page](/en/services/act-therapy.html)
      - baslik: Play and art-based work
        metin: A way of working with children that moves through play, drawing and story rather than talk.
  - type: metin
    metin: |-
      Which approach is used is decided together, according to age, to what is being worked with, and to what the individual prefers. EMDR is not offered at our centre.
  - type: metin
    baslik: How does the process unfold?
    metin: |-
      You do not need to describe the event in detail in a first meeting. First, a foundation is built that supports feeling safe, sleep and day-to-day functioning. Work on the experience itself begins at a pace you can carry. Sessions last 50 minutes and take place weekly or every two weeks. They can be held in Turkish or English, in person or [online](/en/services/online-therapy.html). For more detail, see the [trauma therapy](/en/services/trauma-therapy.html) and [therapy process](/en/therapy-process.html) pages.

      A psychologist does not diagnose. Where a diagnosis or medication is needed, the process is carried out together with a physician. We explain the difference in our article [psychologist or psychiatrist?](/en/blog/psychologist-or-psychiatrist.html)
